 of Elite Sprinters – Verification of the Swing-Pull Technique","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Human Kinetics","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["Sprint-contact instruction should prioritize observable contact placement, active downward and backward foot velocity, posture, and rhythm rather than an unsupported promise of ideal technique.","Use a small task-specific cue set: stay stacked, keep steps short for ankling, contact close to the hips, preserve dorsiflexion without forcing a forefoot strike, let the heel behave naturally, and transition without reaching or locking the knee."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37989833/","sourceTitle":"Ankle and Plantar Flexor Muscle-Tendon Unit Function in Sprinters: A Narrative Review","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":89,"claims":["Sprint plantar-flexor demand changes across start, acceleration, and maximum-velocity phases and should not be represented as uniformly low load.","Stop for foot, shin, calf, Achilles, hamstring, knee, hip, or back pain; loss of balance; repeated reaching or hard heel strike; locked-knee braking; loud or lengthening contacts; cadence drop; asymmetry; ladder displacement; or an unsafe sprint transition."]},{"sectionKey":"programming","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/download?filename=152a49b1-8225-498e-8283-9c580dcd45d9.pdf&urlslug=Women%27s+100m+-+2017+IAAF+World+Championships+Biomechanical+report","sourceTitle":"Women's 100 m — 2017 IAAF World Championships Biomechanical Report","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Sprint drills can progress from simpler to more complex tasks and combine into a sprint transition when the movement time and pattern fit the intended technical model.","Ankling Drill belongs after temperature and tissue preparation and before high-speed sprinting or high-output jumping; use learning versions in a mechanics block and bound or sprint-transition versions in a fresh output block."]},{"sectionKey":"athlete_support","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/personal-best/performance/jereem-richards-games-drills-develop-speed","sourceTitle":"Jereem Richards’ games and drills to help develop speed","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":77,"claims":["Athlete support should show the exact movement at learning and performance cadence, the lane, contact sound, heel behavior, arm action, finish, and how a successful pass differs from merely moving the feet quickly.","Explain that stiffness means controlled resistance to collapse, not locking the knees or forcing the ankle, and show a lower-amplitude or slower-cadence option that preserves the identity."]},{"sectionKey":"coach_support","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8553457/","sourceTitle":"Application of Leg, Vertical, and Joint Stiffness in Running Performance: A Literature Overview","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Healthcare Engineering","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":86,"claims":["Stiffness measures vary by task, speed, fatigue, maturity, and method, so coaches should not infer tendon or joint stiffness from appearance alone.","Provide side and rear observation, contact and distance counts, cadence or timing only when reliably measured, left-right comparison, surface and lane setup, phase placement, regression and progression rules, and a distinction between technical cueing and verified biomechanical measurement."]},{"sectionKey":"accessibility","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/about/position-statements/position_stand_youth_resistance_training---2009.pdf","sourceTitle":"Youth Resistance Training: Updated Position Statement Paper From the NSCA","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Exercise selection and progression should match readiness, technical competence, supervision, equipment scale, and the individual's physical and psychosocial context.","Accessibility options include shorter lanes, slower cadence, supported ankle loading, no-flight ankling, fewer contacts, visual floor marks instead of a raised ladder, quieter settings, extra demonstration, and longer recovery without converting audience context into an exercise-card classification."]},{"sectionKey":"alternates","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/download?filename=152a49b1-8225-498e-8283-9c580dcd45d9.pdf&urlslug=Women%27s+100m+-+2017+IAAF+World+Championships+Biomechanical+report","sourceTitle":"Women's 100 m — 2017 IAAF World Championships Biomechanical Report","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["A straight-leg shuffle into sprint is a compound drill progression, not merely a renamed stationary ankle hop.","Cadence, pass length, contact cap, and coaching intent are delivery annotations; stationary versus traveling, bilateral versus alternating, flight versus no flight, straight-leg bound versus march, ladder constraint, and a terminal sprint may require a controlled variant or distinct definition."]},{"sectionKey":"media","sourceUrl":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/171780?expand=PrivacyEnhancedMode&hl=en","sourceTitle":"Embed videos and playlists","sourcePublisher":"YouTube Help","sourceKind":"manufacturer_instruction","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["YouTube supports privacy-enhanced embedding through youtube-nocookie.com.","The direct candidates for Ankling Drill were discovered through visible YouTube search; current availability and embedding, exact movement identity and variant match, complete viewing, captions, instructional quality, safety, and approval remain separate review gates, and no candidate is approved by this packet."]}],"mediaCandidates":[{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?